  "notes": "Southern Birifor orthography uses ɩ and ʊ for -ATR high vowels. Nasal vowels are marked with a tilde; some are precomposed (ã, ẽ, ĩ, õ, ũ) while others (like ɛ̃, ɔ̃) appear as base vowel + combining tilde. The digraphs 'ch', 'ny', and 'ŋm' represent single phonemes. Tone is generally not marked in the practical orthography, but acute and grave accents may be used in linguistic materials.",
